#Problem APEX 2.1.30

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Conceptual understanding of derivatives)
## KEYWORDS('graph', 'intervals', 'increase', 'decrease')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(5)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('2.1')
## Problem1('30')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

# Set-up

Context("Interval");

$a = random( 2, 9) / 2;
$b = random( 1, 3);
$c = random(-2, 2);

$g = "$a*sin(pi*(x-$c)/(2*$b))";

$xmin = $c - 4 * $b;
$xmax = 2 * $b + $c;

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => $xmin - 1,
    xmax            => $xmax + 1,
    xtick_delta     =>  2,
    ymin            => -5,
    ymax            =>  5,
    rounded_corners => 1
);

$plot->add_function(
    $g, 'x', $xmin, $xmax,
    start_mark => 'circle',
    end_mark   => 'circle',
    color      => 'blue'
);

@c = (
    -4 * $b + $c,
    -3 * $b + $c,
    -2 * $b + $c,
    -$b + $c, $c, $b + $c, 2 * $b + $c
);

$altText =
    "A graph starting with a solid point at ($c[0],0), rising "
    . "to a maximum at ($c[1], $a), passing through the point ($c[2],0), "
    . "falling to a minimum at ($c[3], -$a) passing through the "
    . "point ($c[4],0), rising to a maximum at ($c[5], $a), falling to a "
    . "solid point at ($c[6],0).";

$ans1 = Interval("( $c[0], $c[2] ) U ($c[4], $c[6])");
$ans2 = Interval("( $c[2], $c[4] )");
$ans3 = List($c[0], $c[2], $c[4], $c[6]);
$ans4 = Interval("( $c[0], $c[1] ) U ($c[3], $c[5])");
$ans5 = Interval("( $c[1], $c[3] ) U ($c[5], $c[6])");
$ans6 = List($c[1], $c[3], $c[5]);

# Main text

BEGIN_PGML
Using the graph of [`g(x)`] below, answer the following questions.

>>[![$altText]!]{$plot}{400}<<

a) Where is [` g(x) > 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans1}{10}

b) Where is [` g(x) < 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans2}{10}

c) Where is [` g(x) = 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans3}{10}
 (List all [`x`]-values where [`g`] is zero. Separate multiple values with commas.)

d) Where is [` g'(x) > 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans4}{10}

e) Where is [` g'(x) < 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans5}{10}

f) Where is [` g'(x) = 0`]  ?  [_]{$ans6}{10}
 (List all [`x`]-values where [`g'`] is zero. Separate multiple values with commas.)

[@ helpLink("interval") @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) [` g(x)>0`] are intervals where the points are above the [`x`]-axis.  This is [`[$ans1].`]

b) [` g(x)<0`] are intervals where the points are below the [`x`]-axis.  This is [`[$ans2].`]

c) [` g(x)=0`] are the [`x`]-intercepts or [`[$ans3].`]

d) [`g'(x)>0`] are intervals where the graph is rising or [`[$ans4].`]

e) [`g'(x)<0`] are intervals where the graph is falling or [`[$ans5].`]

f) [`g'(x)=0`] are locations of relative max and mins or [`[$ans6]. `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
